IMPORT prelude

-- Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 2005 (NSW) -- the shared nouns and the reckoning of time (r 1.11), from the seat of the
-- claimant (the plaintiff).
--
-- SEAT: the person bringing a civil claim (a personal injury, compensation, debt or other money claim) in a NSW court:
-- the Supreme Court, the District Court or the Local Court (and, where the rules name them, the Land and Environment Court
-- and the Dust Diseases Tribunal). Every exported question is one that person would ask ("When is the last day ...?").
--
-- r 1.11 governs every period fixed by the rules, by a judgment or order, or by a document in the proceedings. It is
-- used by the questions in ucpr-proceedings.l4 and ucpr-offers-costs.l4. Section 36 of the Interpretation Act 1987 does
-- not apply (r 1.11(5)), so the reckoning here is the rules' own.
--
-- Which days the registry is closed is a fact, not in the rules: the claimant says whether the registry closes on
-- Saturdays and Sundays and lists the other days it is closed (public holidays, the Christmas closure).
--
-- Dates use only the built-in date functions (DATE_FROM_DMY, DATE_SERIAL, DATE_FROM_SERIAL, DATE_DAY, DATE_MONTH,
-- DATE_YEAR), not the daydate library.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 2005 -- time`

§§ `The nouns`

-- The courts the rules apply to that a claimant meets (r 1.5 and the Civil Procedure Act 2005, Sch 1). The Land and
-- Environment Court and the Dust Diseases Tribunal appear only where a rule names them.
DECLARE `Court` IS ONE OF
    `the Supreme Court`
    `the District Court`
    `the Local Court`
    `the Land and Environment Court`
    `the Dust Diseases Tribunal`

-- The days the registry is closed (r 1.11(3), (4)): a fact the claimant states.
DECLARE `Registry closures` HAS
    `closed on Saturdays and Sundays` IS A BOOLEAN
    `other closed days` IS A LIST OF DATE

§§ `Dates`

GIVEN d IS A NUMBER, m IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the date` d m y MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY d m y

GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE, n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the day` `plus days` n MEANS DATE_FROM_SERIAL (DATE_SERIAL `the day` PLUS n)

GIVEN `the start` IS A DATE, `the end` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the days from` `the start` `to` `the end` MEANS DATE_SERIAL `the end` MINUS DATE_SERIAL `the start`

GIVEN `the first day` IS A DATE, `the second day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the first day` `is before` `the second day` IF DATE_SERIAL `the first day` LESS THAN DATE_SERIAL `the second day`

GIVEN `the first day` IS A DATE, `the second day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the first day` `is on or before` `the second day` IF DATE_SERIAL `the first day` AT MOST DATE_SERIAL `the second day`

-- The day of the week from the serial day number: 1 January of year 1 is serial 1, a Monday, so the serial modulo 7 is
-- 6 on a Saturday and 0 on a Sunday.
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a Saturday or Sunday` `the day` IF
        (DATE_SERIAL `the day` MODULO 7) EQUALS 6
    OR  (DATE_SERIAL `the day` MODULO 7) EQUALS 0

GIVEN y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a leap year` y IF
        ((y MODULO 4) EQUALS 0 AND (NOT ((y MODULO 100) EQUALS 0)))
    OR  (y MODULO 400) EQUALS 0

GIVEN m IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of days in month` m y MEANS
    BRANCH IF m EQUALS 2 THEN IF `is a leap year` y THEN 29 ELSE 28
           IF m EQUALS 4 OR m EQUALS 6 OR m EQUALS 9 OR m EQUALS 11 THEN 30
           OTHERWISE 31

-- "n months after" a day: the day with the same number n calendar months later; where that month is too short, its last
-- day (fork F2). The rules do not define a month; r 1.11(5) excludes the Interpretation Act's s 36.
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE, n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the day` `plus months` n MEANS
    `the date` (min (DATE_DAY `the day`) (`the number of days in month` `the new month` `the new year`)) `the new month` `the new year`
    WHERE
        `months counted from year 0` MEANS (DATE_YEAR `the day` TIMES 12) PLUS (DATE_MONTH `the day` MINUS 1) PLUS n
        `the new year` MEANS FLOOR (`months counted from year 0` DIVIDED BY 12)
        `the new month` MEANS (`months counted from year 0` MINUS (`the new year` TIMES 12)) PLUS 1

-- "n months before" a day, the same way.
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE, n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the day` `minus months` n MEANS `the day` `plus months` (0 MINUS n)

§§ `r 1.11 -- reckoning of time`

GIVEN `the closures` IS A `Registry closures`, `the day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the registry is closed under` `the closures` `on` `the day` IF
        (`the closures`'s `closed on Saturdays and Sundays` AND `is a Saturday or Sunday` `the day`)
    OR  elem `the day` (`the closures`'s `other closed days`)

-- r 1.11(4) "If the last day for doing a thing is, or a thing is to be done on, a day on which the registry is closed, the
-- thing may be done on the next day on which the registry is open." The search stops after 60 days (no registry closes
-- for longer).
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE, `the closures` IS A `Registry closures`, `days left to look` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the first open day from` `the day` `under` `the closures` `looking` `days left to look` MEANS
    IF `days left to look` AT MOST 0
    THEN `the day`
    ELSE IF `the registry is closed under` `the closures` `on` `the day`
         THEN `the first open day from` (`the day` `plus days` 1) `under` `the closures` `looking` (`days left to look` MINUS 1)
         ELSE `the day`

GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE, `the closures` IS A `Registry closures`
GIVETH A DATE
`r 1.11(4) moves` `the day` `under` `the closures` MEANS `the first open day from` `the day` `under` `the closures` `looking` 60

-- r 1.11(3) "If, apart from this subrule, the period in question, being a period of 5 days or less, would include a day or
-- part of a day on which the registry is closed, that day is to be excluded." For a short period, only open days count.
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE, `the closures` IS A `Registry closures`, `open days still to count` IS A NUMBER, `days left to look` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`count open days after` `the day` `under` `the closures` `still` `open days still to count` `looking` `days left to look` MEANS
    IF `open days still to count` AT MOST 0 OR `days left to look` AT MOST 0
    THEN `the day`
    ELSE IF `the registry is closed under` `the closures` `on` (`the day` `plus days` 1)
         THEN `count open days after` (`the day` `plus days` 1) `under` `the closures` `still` `open days still to count` `looking` (`days left to look` MINUS 1)
         ELSE `count open days after` (`the day` `plus days` 1) `under` `the closures` `still` (`open days still to count` MINUS 1) `looking` (`days left to look` MINUS 1)

-- r 1.11(2) "If a time of one day or longer is to be reckoned by reference to a given day or event, the given day or the
-- day of the given event is not to be counted." So "n days after" a day ends on the day n days later; with r 1.11(3) for
-- 5 days or less and r 1.11(4) for a closed last day.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER, `the given day` IS A DATE, `the closures` IS A `Registry closures`
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of` n `days after` `the given day` `under` `the closures` MEANS
    IF n AT MOST 5
    THEN `r 1.11(4) moves` (`count open days after` `the given day` `under` `the closures` `still` n `looking` 60) `under` `the closures`
    ELSE `r 1.11(4) moves` (`the given day` `plus days` n) `under` `the closures`

-- "n months after" a day, with r 1.11(4) for a closed last day.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER, `the given day` IS A DATE, `the closures` IS A `Registry closures`
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of` n `months after` `the given day` `under` `the closures` MEANS
    `r 1.11(4) moves` (`the given day` `plus months` n) `under` `the closures`

-- "at least n days before" a day: the given day is not counted (r 1.11(2)), so the last day is n days earlier; r 1.11(4)
-- moves a closed last day to the next open day, as its words say, even though that is fewer than n days before (fork F3).
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER, `the given day` IS A DATE, `the closures` IS A `Registry closures`
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day at least` n `days before` `the given day` `under` `the closures` MEANS
    `r 1.11(4) moves` (`the given day` `plus days` (0 MINUS n)) `under` `the closures`

GIVEN `closed at weekends` IS A BOOLEAN, `the holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A `Registry closures`
`registry closures` `closed at weekends` `the holidays` MEANS
    `Registry closures` WITH
        `closed on Saturdays and Sundays` IS `closed at weekends`
        `other closed days` IS `the holidays`

@export If the rules, a court order or a document in my proceedings give me a number of days after a given day to do something, what is my last day (r 1.11)?
GIVEN `the given day` IS A DATE @desc The day the period runs from (for example the day a document was served on you); it is not counted
      `the number of days` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days the rules, the order or the document give you after that day
      `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays
      `other days the registry is closed` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Every other day in or just after the period on which the registry is closed (public holidays, the Christmas closure)
GIVETH A DATE
`What is my last day` MEANS
    `the last day of` `the number of days` `days after` `the given day` `under` (`registry closures` `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` `other days the registry is closed`)
